{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775255709-6AUKcEZRYTgSSS0KrwQA","role":"assistant","created":1775255756,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand. In the 1979 file, the 1979 data is in the row with:\n`<td colspan=\"3\">1970</td><td colspan=\"3\">1973</td><td colspan=\"3\">1976</td><td colspan=\"3\">1979</td></tr>`\n\nThis means columns 9-11 (7th group) contain the 1979 data.\n\nLet me extract the data correctly.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_02c9afbea8ffb99c","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport json\nimport re\n\n# Function to extract yields for a given year\ndef extract_yields(content, year_row_pattern, year_cols_start):\n    \"\"\"\n    content: table HTML content\n    year_row_pattern: pattern to find the year row\n    year_cols_start: starting column index for the year's data (0-indexed)\n    \"\"\"\n    lines = content.split('<tr>')\n    \n    # Find the year row\n    year_row = None\n    for line in lines:\n        if year_row_pattern in line:\n            year_row = line\n            break\n    \n    if not year_row:\n        return None, None\n    \n    # Find months and extract yields\n    months = ['Jan.', 'Feb.', 'Mar.', 'Apr.', 'May', 'June.', 'July', 'Aug.', 'Sept.', 'Oct.', 'Nov.', 'Dec.']\n    treasury_yields = []\n    corp_yields = []\n    \n    for line in lines:\n        for m in months:\n            if f'<td>{m}</td>' in line or f'<td>{m}</td' in line:\n                tds = re.findall(r'<td>([^<]*)</td>', line)\n                if len(tds) >= year_cols_start + 3:\n                    # Treasury bond yield (first in group)\n                    treasury = tds[year_cols_start].strip()\n                    # Remove footnote markers\n                    treasury = re.sub(r'\\s*\\d+/$', '', treasury)\n                    # Corporate yield (second in group)\n                    corp = tds[year_cols_start + 1].strip()\n                    corp = re.sub(r'\\s*\\d+/$', '', corp)\n                    \n                    try:\n                        treasury_yields.append(float(treasury))\n                        corp_yields.append(float(corp))\n                    except:\n                        print(f\"Error: treasury={treasury}, corp={corp}\")\n                break\n    \n    return treasury_yields, corp_yields\n\n# Extract 1979 data from 1980 bulletin\nd1979 = json.load(open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1980_03.json'))\ne1979 = d1979['document']['elements'][966]\ncontent1979 = e1979.get('content', '')\n\n# 1979 is in the 2nd year row (with 1970, 1973, 1976, 1979)\n# The columns are:\n# Month | 1969(1-3) | 1972(4-6) | 1975(7-9) | 1978(10-12) \n# Wait, let me re-check. Looking at header: Period, Treasury, Aa Corp, Mun (repeated for each year)\n# Each year has 3 columns: Treasury, Aa Corp, Mun\n# So year columns start at: 1, 4, 7, 10... (0-indexed after month)\n# For 1979 which is the 4th year group, columns start at index 10\n\n# Actually wait - the table format is:\n# Period | Year1_Treasury | Year1_AaCorp | Year1_Mun | Year2_Treasury | Year2_AaCorp | Year2_Mun | ...\n# That's 13 columns: 1 (Period) + 4*3 = 13\n\n# For 1979 (4th year group in 1970,1973,1976,1979 row), it's columns 10,11,12 (0-indexed after Period)\n# But since there's a Period column, let's use: index = 1 + 9*1 = 10\n\n# Actually wait - the table shows the year row is:\n# <td colspan=\"3\">1970</td><td colspan=\"3\">1973</td><td colspan=\"3\">1976</td><td colspan=\"3\">1979</td>\n# So each year is 3 columns.\n\n# In the 1970/1973/1976/1979 row, 1979 is the 4th group (3*4=12 columns after period, starting at index 10)\n# Wait no - period is 1 column, so indices are: \n# Period (0), then columns 1-3 (1970), 4-6 (1973), 7-9 (1976), 10-12 (1979)\n# So for 1979: Treasury at index 10, Corporate at index 11\n\n# Actually checking the raw output above, for Jan 1970 we have:\n# ['Jan.', '6.86', '9.00', '6.55', '5.96 4/', '7.61', '4.99', '6.93', '8.97', '6.32']\n# That's 10 cells. So column indices: 0=month, 1-3=1970, 4-6=1973, 7-9=1976\n\n# Wait but 1979 row shows: '<td colspan=\"3\">1970</td><td colspan=\"3\">1973</td><td colspan=\"3\">1976</td><td colspan=\"3\">1979</td>'\n# So 1979 would be columns 10, 11, 12 (0-indexed)\n\n# Let me print what I got more carefully\nlines1979 = content1979.split('<tr>')\nfor i, line in enumerate(lines1979):\n    if '<td>1970</td>' in line and 'colspan' not in line[:50]:\n        print(f\"Line {i}: {line[:400]}\")\n        break\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
